Hi Gabriella,
you don't need to transform your coordinates, or rearrange the order of your lat and long. The function will correctly use Mercator and calculate the distances (which will be in metres) behind the scene. You can use any range intervals you want. That really depends on the questions you are trying to answer (for example, if you are exploring at what distance connectivity drops and you have no previous knowledge, what you suggest makes sense. Alternatively, if you want to test whether you have movements within sites, between sites and between regions, then you may want to set distance classes that reflect this structure) and whether, with the distance classes you use, you have enough data for these to be meaningful. Note that the second column of the table that is returned reports the number of pairwise comparisons. If you have only a few of those, your estimate is probably not accurate. I hope this helps!
carlo

